+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server does not attempt to interpret these relationships: edits are preserved and resolved as complete visits, not combined field by field.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit's current state is its head revision; a visit with competing revisions has unresolved review work until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+## Concurrent Visit Edits: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Every visit carries a revision chain. Revision IDs are server-assigned and monotonic per visit; the first submission creates revision 0, and each accepted edit appends one revision. A stored revision is immutable: neither its payload nor its author, receipt time, or base revision pointer is ever rewritten.
+
+### Submission envelope
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visit UUID | Client-generated, stable across the visit's lifetime |
+| plot ID | Catalog plot reference, validated at submission |
+| base revision ID | The head revision the edit was made against; the initial submission names no base |
+| payload | The complete visit field set as the worker submitted it |
+| author ID | Authenticated submitting worker |
+| idempotency key | Client-generated; makes retries safe |
+| client timestamp | When the worker made the edit, for review context |
+
+A submission with a base revision equal to the visit's current head is accepted and appended as the new head. A submission whose base revision is not the head — a concurrent edit — is stored as a **competing revision**: retained in full, never applied to current state, and the visit is marked as having unresolved review work. The server never merges, reorders, or selects between competing revisions on its own.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+### Visit states and retrieval
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A visit is either **current** (its head is the only revision not superseded or resolved against) or **unresolved** (competing revisions exist). Retrieval returns the head revision and the status; when unresolved, it also returns the competing revisions so a caller can show what conflicts. No auto-selection, no defaults, no partial application. Which of these a caller may export is review-export's decision, sized around the unresolved-work representation here.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+### Resolution
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+A coordinator resolution names one competing revision as the outcome. The selected revision becomes the head; losing revisions remain stored as history. The resolution records the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs, and the outcome. Resolution is the only operation that removes a visit's unresolved status; there is no automatic timeout or lapse.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Whether a coordinator may also record a manually composed payload as the resolution outcome — rather than selecting one competing revision — is an open question deferred to review-export's design, since it sizes the coordinator interface; if it is allowed, the composed payload cites the contributing submissions and loses nothing. Until review-export settles it, resolution selects one revision.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The submission envelope above is the contract tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around: tablets retain the base revision ID of each pending edit and re-send whole payloads on retry. The unresolved-work representation is the contract review-export sizes its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around: a revision-comparison view lists a visit's competing revisions; unresolved visits stay out of the export set. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions resolved by a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge with automatic combination of disjoint field edits | The team chose a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits (e.g., a species identification and its cover estimate) that field names alone cannot reveal. The server cannot judge which field edits are scientifically linked, so automatic combination risks producing records nobody submitted. Whole-visit revisions keep every submitted visit intact, matching the tenet against silently discarding edits. Tablet-sync keeps only base revision IDs instead of base snapshots, and review-export gets a simpler, uniform comparison screen. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions over field-aware three-way merge (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether a coordinator resolution may record a manually composed payload rather than selecting one competing revision — decided during review-export design; until then resolution selects one revision.
+2. Resolution worklist shape and assignment between the two coordinators — belongs to review-export.
